---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Omnisend vs Postscript for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Omnisend and Postscript through channel availability, proposed Shopify drop tests, permission, migration controls and matched campaign economics.';
const faqs = [{"question": "Which should a Shopify merchant test first?", "answer": "Test Omnisend when one team wants to coordinate email, SMS and eligible web push. Test Postscript when its Shopify SMS workflows suit the release calendar; verify access to its announced Email product before including that product in a migration plan."}, {"question": "Is Postscript still SMS-only?", "answer": "No. Postscript has announced Email. Its public pricing page links an Email waitlist, so confirm account access, enabled workflows, migration support and the written quote before relying on it."}, {"question": "Does Omnisend include email and SMS?", "answer": "Omnisend offers email, SMS/MMS and web push. Sending eligibility, channel permission, plan limits and SMS charges still need checking for the account and recipient countries."}, {"question": "Which is better for limited drops?", "answer": "Either is a candidate if a pilot proves inventory checks, approved audience selection, purchase exits and queue retirement. A marketing workflow does not reserve stock or guarantee checkout priority."}, {"question": "Must either be paired with Sequenzy?", "answer": "No. Keep an existing email provider if it meets the job, or compare a replacement on a defined requirement. Add Sequenzy or another provider only when its incremental benefit exceeds migration, integration and coordination costs."}, {"question": "Can both run during migration?", "answer": "A controlled overlap can work with one owner per message job and a shared suppression process. Disable old triggers and retire queued sends before moving ownership; do not replay historical abandonment events."}, {"question": "How should permission transfer?", "answer": "Preserve channel-specific permission source, timestamp, disclosure and opt-out evidence. A phone number, purchase, waitlist entry or email subscription alone does not establish SMS marketing permission. Hold ambiguous records for review."}, {"question": "What happens after a purchase or opt-out?", "answer": "Use canonical Shopify customer and order records to stop obsolete promotions, cancel queued reminders and apply the correct channel suppression. Test delayed events, retries and merged profiles before scaling."}, {"question": "How should costs be compared?", "answer": "Use matched eligible cohorts and a holdout, net sales after discounts and returns, product cost, fulfillment, messaging, platform fees and support. Compare contribution per eligible customer at the same volume and horizon, rather than provider-attributed revenue."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Omnisend vs Postscript</span></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Omnisend vs Postscript for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose around the campaign job, available channels and the team's ability to govern sends. Omnisend offers email, SMS and eligible web push; Postscript offers Shopify SMS and has announced Email. Confirm Email access before planning a Postscript email cutover.</p>
      <p class="mt-5">The merchants below are fictional proposed test fixtures, not customer evidence, measured outcomes or rankings.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[
      { label: 'Coordinated channels', tool: 'Omnisend', reason: 'A candidate when one team owns email, SMS and eligible web push' },
      { label: 'Shopify SMS operations', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'A candidate when its available workflows fit your release and support process' },
      { label: 'Email migration', tool: 'Confirm availability', reason: 'Postscript Email is announced; validate account access and scope before cutover' },
    ]} /></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Current supplier scope and availability</h2>
      <p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.omnisend.com/pricing/">Omnisend pricing</a> lists email, SMS/MMS and web push, with channel limits by plan. Web push requires a supported browser/device and its own permission; it is not a substitute for SMS consent. <a class="link" href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/2747083-get-started-with-sms-channel">Omnisend's SMS guide</a> distinguishes channel subscription statuses and recipient-country and account eligibility.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/launch">Postscript's launch page</a> announces Email and <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">its Email page</a> describes the product. The <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/pricing">Postscript pricing page</a> links an Email waitlist. Treat availability as account-specific: request confirmation of enabled features, deliverability setup, event coverage, migration assistance and contractual pricing. Neither an announcement nor a waitlist proves general access for your store.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/1832295-manage-your-omnisend-sms-credits-subscription">Omnisend's SMS billing guide</a> describes current Pro access through separately purchased SMS credits; current Free and Standard do not offer SMS subscriptions, except qualifying continuously active legacy setups predating May 4, 2026. Legacy Pro was retired October 26, 2026. Do not assume an older Pro credit allowance applies to a new account. Obtain a current quote for plan fees, separate SMS credits, destination mix, message segments, MMS, carrier charges, overages and legacy exceptions. Request the same scope from Postscript; supplier savings claims are not a matched cost comparison.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Omnisend test</th><th>Postscript test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Channel ownership</td><td>Coordinate supported email, SMS and push with explicit permission</td><td>Validate SMS operations; include Email only after account access is confirmed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Limited release</td><td>Prove segment, stock and purchase-exit behavior</td><td>Prove the same behavior with available workflows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Support burden</td><td>Test reply routing and escalation with the team</td><td>Test reply routing and escalation with the same staffing assumptions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Economics</td><td>Quote the same eligible audience and send mix</td><td>Quote the same eligible audience and send mix</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <h2>Proposed fictional drop and audience tests</h2>
      <p>Each fixture needs separately evidenced channel permission, current Shopify inventory and a named campaign owner. Thresholds are proposed rules to validate, not proven best practices or vendor-native guarantees.</p>
      <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: collector records</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Disclose pressing quantity and test early access for opted-in waitlist members with two prior orders. Treat a proposed $400 lifetime-value threshold as a hypothesis; account for refunds and merged profiles before assigning it.</p>
      <h3>Arena Energy: sports nutrition</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Separate flavor-drop timing from the optional shaker offer. Test interest-based audiences for the athlete collaboration; approve product and offer claims before either channel sends.</p>
      <h3>Loom & Frame: limited art prints</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Publish edition size and test a reminder to eligible three-order collectors only if verified sell-through reaches the proposed halfway threshold. Cancel if inventory or edition metadata is stale.</p>
      <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: seasonal boardshorts</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Use email for swell-forecast editorial and a separately opted-in restock audience for SMS. Confirm size and SKU availability before releasing a reminder.</p>
      <h3>Noir Collective: streetwear</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Compare a proposed Thursday drop with a two-hour VIP window and a lower broadcast frequency. Keep a holdout to measure whether restraint changes contribution and complaints.</p>
      <h3>SoleDrop ATX: limited sneakers</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Test a size-specific waitlist and early link. A link is not a stock reservation: checkout must enforce allocation, and general announcements should depend on live remaining sizes.</p>
      <h3>Festival Threads Co: tour merchandise</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Match venue interest to city and recipient timezone. Test a proposed three-text cap per city; exclude people without a reliable local sending window.</p>
      <h3>Bloom & Wick: candle gifting</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Separate gift deadlines and bundle offers from self-purchase scent education. Stop deadline reminders when fulfillment can no longer meet the stated promise.</p>
      <h3>Peak Trail Gear: outdoor clearance</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Test clearance offers among eligible price-sensitive customers with a holdout. Keep full-price loyalists separate until incremental margin supports extending the offer.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Lab: small-batch hot sauce</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Test one launch and at most one inventory reminder. A proposed 200-bottle threshold must come from current sellable stock, with cancellation if the batch sells out.</p>
      <h3>Ember Ceramics: maker pottery</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Use a hypothetical 4,200-person waitlist for an 80-piece kiln release to test allocation pressure. Preserve fulfillment order in Shopify; neither sender should imply everyone on the list can buy.</p>
      <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: subscriptions</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Exclude active subscribers from acquisition flash offers using current subscription status. Test late renewal and cancellation events before resuming eligibility.</p>
      <h3>Cipher Audio: high-value headphones</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Test a two-week educational email sequence and a launch text for eligible interested shoppers. Compare a no-discount offer against a matched alternative without asserting a margin lift.</p>
      <h3>Patchwork Denim: one-of-one clothing</h3>
      <p>Proposed fictional fixture. Match style preferences and sizes to individual listings. Retire queued promotions immediately when the only item sells, and avoid redirecting a sold-item reminder to an unrelated product.</p>
      <h2>Permission, identity and order truth</h2>
      <p>Use the canonical Shopify customer ID and order ID, with documented handling for guest checkout, phone normalization, shared numbers and profile merges. Do not merge solely on an ambiguous number. Keep permission evidence per channel: capture source, timestamp, disclosure version and scope, plus opt-out history. Waitlist interest and high customer value never override permission. Quarantine uncertain imports and resolve them before sending.</p>
      <p>Record the event ID, event time and ingestion time. Reconcile purchases, cancellations, refunds, subscription status and current sellable stock against Shopify before dispatch. Purchase exits must cancel obsolete cart reminders and queued release nudges across the responsible providers; a segment refresh alone is insufficient proof. Use a deduplication key for customer, campaign job, channel and event so webhook retries, delayed events and replays cannot create a second send. Test a purchase arriving after scheduling but before dispatch and a retry arriving after cancellation.</p>
      <h2>Approvals, handoff and rollback</h2>
      <p>Assign one owner per message job, with a shared calendar and contact-pressure policy. Review audience counts, exclusion reasons, inventory, offer terms, links, channel permission and recipient-local sending windows before approval. Test opt-out and help handling on every active sender, including failure and support escalation paths. Route replies to a staffed queue with a named handoff owner; pause promotions when complaints or fulfillment questions exceed the agreed threshold.</p>
      <p>For migration, export permission and suppression evidence, reconcile counts, then pilot a small eligible cohort. Disable the old trigger and explicitly cancel its queued sends before enabling the new owner. Record the cutover timestamp and do not replay historical cart events. If purchase exits, identity mapping or reply routing fail, pause new sends, retire the new queue, reconcile events and suppressions, and restore only the approved old jobs. Rollback must not resurrect opt-outs or duplicate reminders.</p>
      <h2>Matched hypothetical campaign economics</h2>
      <p>This illustration is not a provider quote or forecast. For either candidate, assign 10,000 eligible customers to treatment and 10,000 comparable customers to holdout over the same observation window. Suppose treatment net sales are $12,000 and holdout net sales are $10,000, after discounts and returns. The $2,000 difference is the hypothetical incremental net sales; do not subtract those discounts and returns again.</p>
      <p>Subtract $800 incremental product cost, $200 incremental fulfillment, $250 messaging, $150 allocated platform cost and $100 incremental support. Contribution is $2,000 - $800 - $200 - $250 - $150 - $100 = $500, or $0.05 per eligible treatment customer. The same inputs produce the same result for Omnisend and Postscript. If messaging doubles to $500, contribution is $250, or $0.025 per eligible customer. Replace every assumption with matched pilot observations and written quotes, including migration labor and any retained email provider. Provider attribution and revenue per delivered message cannot replace a comparable holdout.</p>
      <h2>Conditional fit and next decision</h2>
      <p>Omnisend is a positive candidate when a team values coordinated supported channels and can prove the required controls. Postscript is a positive candidate when its Shopify SMS operations and support process meet the release job; its announced Email deserves evaluation where access and required features are confirmed. Neither is a mandatory graduation path from the other.</p>
      <p>Retaining an existing email service can be reasonable. Adding <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> or another service requires a specific unmet job and a costed handoff; there is no default second email layer. Compare the <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> profiles, then define a pilot using <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">permission records</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>. Select after the controls and matched economics pass.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Omnisend vs Postscript FAQ" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
